body:
Source: Fable security review .krn/runs/arch-review/security.md (items 2, 3, 5, 8, 9, 10, 12-18) and history class 6 (isolation bypass: 6 fixes / 1582 lines).
Evidence: the write guard in src/hook/scope.ts judges shell with regexes (three heredoc strippers), a list of known write commands and lexical paths. Bypasses that remain after t-1kt1vb9:
- a lane grants itself a lane-target with `dd of=tasks/<id>.json` (scope.ts:91-101);
- a symlink inside the worktree points outside it (332-345);
- a lead-session subagent writes to other repositories (331, 276);
- cp -t, env cp, /bin/cp, ln, install, dd of=, bash -c and git --git-dir (228-247);
- pushd and subshell cd (216);
- `<<<` or `$((1<<X))` hides the rest of a command (166, 177);
- Codex has no write guard (src/dispatch/table.ts:123-133).
Files: src/hook/scope.ts, src/dispatch/table.ts, test/hook/scope*.test.ts
Outcome: a single path oracle: realpathSync.native of the nearest existing ancestor joined with the lexical rest. Unknown write-capable shell commands fail closed in lane sessions. The lane-target can come only from the lead's environment, never from a file the lane can write. Codex gets the same guard.
Proof: a property test checks that ALLOW implies the target is inside an allowed base for file tools. A differential test runs each allowed Bash command in a temp tree and asserts that nothing outside the allowed bases changed.
